WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.800
Justice League, we hardly knew ye.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.299
Filmmaker, writer, and comic book superfan
Kevin Smith recently revealed on his Fatman

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:12.750
Beyond podcast that Zack Snyder's 2017 film
was meant to be the first in a trilogy — and

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.660
he also offered up a bunch of juicy details
on the movies that might have been.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.860
According to Smith, his inside knowledge is
a result of a visit he paid to the set of

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.100
the upcoming, Star Wars: Episode IX.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.100
During that visit, he had a chance to chat
with a number of unnamed crew members who

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.180
worked on, as he put it, "[...] both versions
of Justice League."

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:34.280
That refers to both Justice League as it was
originally conceived under Snyder, as well

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.220
as the reshot and reworked final version overseen
by The Avengers director Joss Whedon.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.640
"They called it Justice League when Snyder
was doing it — Zack Snyder.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.160
When Joss Whedon came in and took over, they
called it Josstice League."

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:53.291
Whedon took over the production of Justice
League when Snyder was forced to depart, due

00:00:53.291 --> 00:00:54.410
to a family tragedy.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:58.399
But apparently, Whedon did much more than
just finish the movie — as many fans have

00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:03.360
long suspected, he also oversaw a dramatic
overhauling of what the movie was about, and

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.360
what it was building to.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.780
Smith asserted on the January 29th episode
of his podcast that Snyder's version of Justice

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.580
League was originally meant to build toward
not just one, but rather two sequels.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.600
According to Smith, the first movie was meant
to proceed much like the finished version

00:01:17.609 --> 00:01:22.939
audiences saw in 2017, following the newly-formed
League as they met and fought the New God

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:23.440
Steppenwolf.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.600
However, the ending of the movie as originally
conceived would have revealed the encounter

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.240
to be just a prelude to the real fight to
come.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.780
Smith asserted that Justice League was originally
supposed to end with a sequence revealing

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.240
the ultimate villain to be not Steppenwolf,
but rather Darkseid.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.540
The heroes would encounter the infamous DC
villain viewing the film's events through

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.799
a Boom Tube, a sort of extra-dimensional tunnel
which can be opened with the aid of a Mother

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.300
Box.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:54.620
"Boom tube opened up, and they saw Darkseid,
and Darkseid saw them."

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.500
“Boom tube closes, and that's the end of
the f------' movie."

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.759
This would have led directly into a sequel,
in which the Justice League would've become

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.360
aware of the growing threat posed by Darkseid
and his alien minions.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.400
According to Smith, the movie would have sent
the Justice League into outer space, taking

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.220
the fight directly to the intergalactic despot's
home planet of Apokolips.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.670
The second film would have brought in several
of DC Comics' more cosmic elements, including

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.640
the Green Lantern Corps, with some version
of Green Lantern himself joining the fight.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.240
Apparently, the scene from a Justice League
Comic-Con trailer, which featured Jeremy Irons'

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:30.880
Alfred addressing an unknown person, was meant
to feature the Dark Knight's butler interacting

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:31.920
with Green Lantern.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.620
The scene — and the Green Lantern — was
cut from the film when Whedon took over.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:39.500
"He said you'd come.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.580
Now… let's hope you're not too late."

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.650
Smith acknowledged that many have assumed
the scene was meant to involve a revived Superman,

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.750
but according to the filmmaker's sources,
that wasn't always the case.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:03:00.080
"'We had green lights that were flashing on
Jeremy Irons to project a green glow.'

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:01.500
I said, 'Why?'

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.640
He goes, 'Because it was Green Lantern.'"

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:08.160
According to Smith, the proposed second movie
would have ended on a dark note in the vein

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:12.860
of Avengers: Infinity War or The Empire Strikes
Back, with the heroes of the Justice League

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:16.580
separated, stranded and otherwise defeated
by Darkseid and his minions.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:21.240
The movie would have ended with Darkseid arriving
on Earth, causing mass destruction and ending

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.740
the film on a hopeless note.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:27.520
The third movie would have finally shown audiences
the reality of the apocalyptic vision experienced

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:31.720
by Bruce Wayne in Batman V. Superman's strange
"Knightmare" sequence.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.730
The trilogy would have ultimately concluded
with the Justice League reunited, returning

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:39.520
to Earth to make their final stand against
Darkseid and the armies of Apokolips.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:44.720
Ever since the poor critical performance and
muted box office turnout for Batman v Superman,

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.760
Warner Bros. has been pivoting away from Snyder's
vision for the DC movie universe.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.660
Many fans were left unsatisfied by how Justice
League ended up turning out, but Smith's report

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:57.610
suggests that there was originally a strong
vision for the project, one which was close

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:58.520
to coming to fruition.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:03.460
But since Justice League entered production
so soon after the release of Batman v Superman,

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:08.170
course corrections were made on the fly, dramatically
altering the movie from the form it was supposedly

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:08.880
meant to take.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:13.200
We may not ever fully know what went on during
the making of Justice League, but every story

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:17.659
that's emerged from the production suggests
a movie that went through wild changes, basically

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:18.580
at the last minute.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.300
Whether Smith's account of events is accurate
or not, it's only more fuel for the fire for

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.560
fans still demanding that WB "Release the
Snyder Cut."

